many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism this is a a  commonly asked question and it's a good  one why do the Stoics only recognize  four virtues it seems like there's a lot  of other virtues that they're leaving  off of their list so what are the four  virtues that the Stoics do in fact  recognize they're often called the  cardinal virtues and these are wisdom  justice courage and temperance so why  didn't the Stoics talk about all sorts  of other virtues like great-souled  Nisour control of anger or generosity or  honesty so there's a good answer to this  there's a very simple and  straightforward answer to this which is  that the Stoics did in fact talk about a  whole bunch of other virtues and they  grouped them under these four primary  virtues now when you hear that answer  you might say what wait a second dr.  Sandler I've looked at a lot of stoic  blog posts and podcasts and websites and  books and I've only seen them talk about  four virtues and I know that other  virtue ethicists talk about a whole  range of other virtues so so why is this  confusion arising well that's a good  secondary question the answer to that  has to do with a little bit of history  we only possess a small selection of  what it is that classic stoic authors  wrote and what we have is what we call  late stoicism which are the writers  during the Roman Empire who for the most  part are assuming an audience that's  very conversant with the existing stoic  literature that we don't have access to  in that existing stoic literature as we  know from other texts the Stoics did in  fact have these four main virtues which  they went into great explanation about  some of those explanations included  telling us which other Verte  shoes fell under those so because  contemporary stoic literature for the  most part is drawing on sources like  Epictetus marcus aurelius seneca we're  not actually getting a detailed  discussion of all these other virtues  although they mention them quite  frequently now if you want to know more  about how the Stoics understood the  entire range of the virtues where would  you go there's three classic source  texts that would be very useful for you  and these are often considered you might  say Advanced Study in stoicism so a lot  of beginners don't encounter these one  of them is deities Laertes lives of the  Philosopher's which has an entire book  on the stoic school where he gives a  summary of the stoic doctrines and he  talks about the primary virtues and then  some of the secondary virtues another  would be arias did a mrs. epitome of  stoic ethics that I'll read from in just  a moment where he summarizes what the  stoic teaching is on these as well and  gives you a listing the third source  that I think would be particularly  useful would be going to Cicero  particularly on duties although you can  find this in some of his other works  where he talks about what the virtues  what these four cardinal virtues of  wisdom justice temperance and courage  are really all about and you can see  that they cover a very wide range  including things that other virtue  ethics recognized as separate virtue in  arias didymus is summary or epitome of  stoic ethics he tells us that there are  these four primary virtues and then  there's a number of other virtues that  can be grouped under these primary  virtues so he tells us to wisdom our  subordinated soundness of judgment  circumspection shrewdness sensibleness  soundness of aim and ingenuity so  each of these is a intellectual virtue  you might say to temperance or  self-restraint  are subordinated orderliness propriety  modesty and self-control for courage or  bravery are subordinated perseverance  intrepid nice great heartedness stout  hardness and industriousness and finally  to justice subordinated piety kindness  good fellowship and fair dealing  doesn't that list cover every possible  disposition that might be called a  virtue in some other moral theory no but  if we dig around in the stomach text we  will find a lot of other things that we  consider to be virtues it might be  missing on the stoic list to actually be  there talked about by say epictetus or  Marcus Aurelius or Seneca as part of  virtue if we read through Cicero's works  this is also particularly helpful  because you see him assimilating many  other things to stoic virtue to bring  this to a close the Stoics do in fact  talk about a number of virtues it's just  that they do so in a way that gives  primacy to these four cardinal virtues  of wisdom justice temperance and courage  and that arranges the other virtues  underneath them so there aren't any  virtues actually missing in the stoic  schema it's just a matter of how we  approach the text and so there's a bit  of a historical reason for why these  confusions arise if you found this  answer helpful for you then please share  it with others who might benefit from it  this work is entirely supported by  crowdfunding on my patreon site so if  you find it valuable consider becoming a  supporter at patreon.com slash Sadler
 

